    "q-352679": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352679",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we select the identity that contains a ${tan}^{2}⁡x$ term. So, we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-1={tan}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{3/2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{\\,3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
